window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["ta"]={"tatlas": [[null, ["Hi, Buddy here. This sheet is about technical analysis: reading price and volume to judge where a stock or market is likely to go next, where you'd be wrong, and how much to risk. Before we start, one honest note. This is an educational reference, not investment advice, and trading carries a real risk of loss. Technical analysis doesn't predict the future; it helps you put the odds slightly on your side and, more importantly, control the damage when you're wrong. Let's look at the map."]], ["technical-analysis-topic-atlas", ["The atlas has eight areas. Market mechanics: the order book, the participants, spreads and slippage, order types and sessions. Trend and cycles: Dow theory, the four stages a stock goes through, multiple timeframes, and trend versus range. Price action: support and resistance, trendlines, market structure and liquidity grabs. Candles and patterns: candle anatomy, one, two and three candle patterns, reversal and continuation chart patterns, breakouts, gaps and pattern statistics. Indicators: trend, momentum, volatility and volume tools, divergences, Fibonacci and Elliott waves. Advanced topics: smart money concepts, Wyckoff, intermarket analysis, breadth and sentiment. Trading: strategy styles, a setup library, CAN SLIM, risk and position sizing, and psychology. And testing and automation: backtesting pitfalls, walk forward testing, quant strategies and Python code."]], ["how-to-use-this-sheet", ["If you're learning, read market mechanics, trend and cycles, and price action first, because every indicator and pattern is just a summary of price and volume. To build a method, pick one strategy, one or two indicators and the risk rules, and write them down as a trading plan. Before a trade, run the top down flow, size the position, and use the pre trade checklist at the end. And to improve, journal every trade, backtest rule changes, and change only one thing at a time."]], ["top-down-analysis-flow", ["This flow is how a trade gets built, top down. Start with the higher timeframe trend. Mark the key levels. Decide the regime: is the market trending or ranging? Choose a setup that fits that regime. Wait for an entry trigger. Then set the stop and position size, manage the trade with partial profits and a trailing stop, and finally journal and review it. The important idea is that each step can veto the trade. If you can't name the level, the stop or the size, there is no trade.", "The takeaway: Higher-timeframe trend, key levels, regime, a setup that fits, a trigger, stop and size, then manage.", "Pause and answer: What comes right after the higher-timeframe trend?", "⏸", "Answer: Marking the key levels."]], ["the-three-questions-behind-every-chart", ["Every chart answers three questions. First, which way is the path of least resistance? You judge that from structure, meaning higher highs and higher lows, moving averages, the higher timeframe trend and the market stage. Second, where would I be wrong? That comes from support and resistance, swing points, pattern boundaries and the average true range. And third, is the move supported? Volume, breadth, momentum divergence and confirmation from related markets answer that. If you can answer all three, you understand the chart.", "The takeaway: Which way is least resistance, where would I be wrong, and is the reward worth the risk?", "Pause and answer: Which tools answer 'where would I be wrong?'", "⏸", "Answer: Support and resistance and swing points: they place the stop."]]], "basics": [[null, ["Let's start with market mechanics: how price actually moves. This is the foundation that most beginners skip."]], ["price-discovery-and-the-order-book", ["Here's the key insight: price is just the last trade, not fair value. It moves when aggressive orders use up the liquidity resting at the best price. Look at the example. Buyers have bids at fifty, forty nine ninety nine and forty nine ninety eight. Sellers have asks of two hundred shares at fifty point zero one, a hundred and fifty at fifty point zero two, and four hundred at fifty point zero three. Now a market buy order for three hundred and fifty shares arrives. It takes all two hundred shares at fifty point zero one, then all one hundred and fifty at fifty point zero two. The best ask is now fifty point zero three, so one order moved the price two ticks. That's why large investors slice their orders over time with algorithms like TWAP and VWAP, and why a thin order book lets a single order spike the price. Liquidity is the ability to trade size without moving the price: thick books mean tight spreads and little slippage.", "The takeaway: Price is the last trade, not fair value; it moves when aggressive orders exhaust the liquidity at the best price.", "Pause and answer: What makes price move?", "⏸", "Answer: Aggressive orders exhausting the liquidity resting at the best price."]], ["who-is-on-the-other-side", ["Every trade has someone on the other side, so know who's there. Retail traders trade small and react to news and social media. Institutions like mutual funds chase returns against a benchmark, and they leave footprints: steady buying or selling over days, and heavy volume at key levels. Market makers earn the spread by quoting both sides, and they widen or pull their quotes in stress. High frequency algorithms keep spreads tight in calm markets, but their liquidity can vanish in a shock. Hedgers offset business risk and trade large amounts regardless of price. And central banks move rarely, but overwhelmingly, in rates and currencies.", "The takeaway: Retail reacts, institutions accumulate or distribute over days, and market makers and algos provide liquidity.", "Pause and answer: What's the chart footprint of institutional buying?", "⏸", "Answer: Steady accumulation over days, with volume at key levels."]], ["bid-ask-spread-and-slippage", ["The bid is the best price buyers will pay; the ask is the best price sellers will accept; the spread is the difference, the cost of buying and immediately selling. Slippage is getting filled worse than the quoted price, because there wasn't enough size at the quote, the market was moving fast, or your order was large. Spreads widen with low volume, news, small companies and off hours. Budget at least half the spread on both entry and exit. For scalpers trading tiny moves in illiquid names, the spread alone can erase the whole edge.", "The takeaway: The spread is the cost of an instant round trip; slippage is a fill worse than the quote, and both widen in thin or fast markets.", "Pause and answer: Define the spread.", "⏸", "Answer: Ask − bid: the cost of buying and instantly selling."]], ["order-types", ["Order types. A market order fills immediately at the best available price, so use it when speed matters more than price, accepting slippage. A limit order fills at your price or better, good for entries at levels and exits at targets, but it may never fill. A stop order becomes a market order when the trigger price is hit; it's used for stop losses and breakout entries, but if the price gaps past your stop, you get filled far beyond it. A stop limit becomes a limit order at the trigger, which caps the price but has a nasty failure: in a gap it may not fill at all, and you stay stuck in the losing trade. And bracket or one cancels other orders attach a stop and a target to your entry automatically.", "The takeaway: Market for speed, limit for price, stop to force an exit, stop-limit for a price floor that may not fill.", "Pause and answer: What's the risk of a limit order?", "⏸", "Answer: It may never fill."]], ["sessions-and-timing", ["Timing matters. Indian cash markets trade from nine fifteen in the morning to three thirty in the afternoon, with a pre open session from nine to nine fifteen. The opening gap and first fifteen to thirty minutes are noisy, and the closing period carries heavy volume. US stocks trade from nine thirty to four, Eastern time, with most volume at the open and close and a quiet drift at midday. In currencies, the London and New York overlap is the most liquid window. Crypto trades all week, but liquidity still clusters around stock market hours, and weekends bring big wicks. Intraday, mark the open, the prior day's high and low, and VWAP. And cut size or stand aside during thin hours and before scheduled news.", "The takeaway: Opens and closes carry the most volume; India's first 15–30 minutes are noisy.", "Pause and answer: Indian cash market hours?", "⏸", "Answer: 9:15 am to 3:30 pm IST, with pre-open from 9:00."]], ["microstructure-in-one-card", ["Microstructure in one card. Orders are filled by price, then time: the best price fills first, and at the same price, earlier orders fill first. Limit orders that rest in the book are makers that add liquidity, often at lower fees; market orders are takers. Gaps form when news arrives while the market is closed, which is why stops can fill far away. Trades happen on exchanges, electronic networks, dealers and dark pools for block trades. And the lesson: as a retail trader, your edge is in choosing setups and controlling risk, never in speed.", "The takeaway: Price-time priority, makers add liquidity, takers remove it, and gaps form while the market is closed.", "Pause and answer: Price-time priority means what?", "⏸", "Answer: Best price fills first; at the same price, earlier orders first."]], ["india-market-specifics", ["Some India specific rules. Cash equities settle the next trading day, T plus one. In the pre open session, orders are collected from nine to about nine eight, and a call auction sets the opening price. The official NSE close isn't the last trade either. From the third of August twenty twenty six, stocks that have derivatives close through a closing auction: continuous trading stops at three fifteen, and the auction sets the closing price by three thirty five, within three percent of the average price from three o'clock to three fifteen. Other stocks still close at the volume weighted average price of the last thirty minutes. Stocks outside the derivatives segment have daily price bands, or circuits, of two, five, ten or twenty percent, while F and O stocks have wider dynamic bands. If the Nifty or Sensex moves ten, fifteen or twenty percent, market wide circuit breakers halt trading. If a stock's open interest exceeds ninety five percent of its market wide position limit, it enters the F and O ban, and no fresh positions are allowed. Costs include brokerage, securities transaction tax, exchange fees, GST and stamp duty, and they add up fast for intraday and F and O traders. One practical warning: a stock locked at its lower circuit can't be sold at all, so your stop won't save you. Size small in names with tight bands.", "The takeaway: T+1 settlement, a 9:00–9:15 pre-open call auction, and an official close that isn't simply the last trade.", "Pause and answer: Settlement cycle for Indian cash equities?", "⏸", "Answer: T+1."]], ["asset-classes-compared", ["Different markets behave differently. Equities trade in sessions with low to moderate leverage, driven by earnings, sector rotation and macro, and they gap overnight. Index futures and options carry high leverage and have expiry effects and open interest to watch. Forex trades all week day and night with high leverage, driven by interest rate differentials and central banks, and respects session ranges and round numbers. Commodities are driven by supply and demand, the US dollar and real yields, with contract rolls and seasonality. And crypto trades all the time with very high leverage, driven by sentiment and flows, with big wicks in thin weekend markets.", "The takeaway: Equities gap overnight, futures and options carry leverage and expiries, FX trades nearly round the clock.", "Pause and answer: Which chart quirk matters most for equities?", "⏸", "Answer: Overnight gaps."]]], "dow": [[null, ["This section is about trend and market cycles: the big picture that should decide which direction you trade."]], ["dow-theory-six-tenets", ["Dow theory is over a hundred years old, and its six tenets still underpin technical analysis. One: the averages discount everything, so all known information is in the price. Two: there are three movements: the primary trend lasting a year or more, secondary reactions of weeks to months that retrace a third to two thirds of the prior move, and minor moves lasting days. Three: primary trends have three phases. In a bull market: accumulation by informed buyers, public participation, then excess. Bear markets mirror it: distribution, public selling and panic. Four: the averages must confirm each other; originally, industrials and transports. Five: volume confirms the trend, expanding in the direction of the primary trend. And six: a trend persists until there's a clear reversal, meaning the sequence of higher highs and higher lows breaks.", "The takeaway: The averages discount everything, three movements run at once, primary trends have phases, averages and volume must confirm.", "Pause and answer: How much does a secondary reaction usually retrace?", "⏸", "Answer: About a third to two-thirds."]], ["the-four-stages-of-a-stock", ["Stan Weinstein's four stages describe a stock's life cycle. Stage one is accumulation, a base: sideways movement after a decline, a flat thirty week moving average and drying volume. Watch it, and buy the breakout from the base. Stage two is markup: higher highs and lows, a rising moving average and volume on up moves. Hold and add on pullbacks. Stage three is distribution, a top: sideways after an advance, the average flattening and wide swings. Tighten stops and take profits. Stage four is markdown: lower highs and lows and a falling average. Avoid buying. Most money is made in stage two, and most losses come from buying stage four because it \"looks cheap\".", "The takeaway: Accumulation, markup, distribution, markdown: buy breakouts from stage 1, hold stage 2, avoid 3 and 4.", "Pause and answer: What does stage 1 look like?", "⏸", "Answer: Sideways after a decline, a flat 30-week MA and drying volume."]], ["stage-cycle", ["This little diagram shows the cycle: accumulation leads to markup, markup to distribution, distribution to markdown, and markdown eventually back into a new accumulation base. Before entering any trade, ask yourself which stage the stock is in.", "The takeaway: Stage 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → back to 1.", "Pause and answer: Which stage follows distribution?", "⏸", "Answer: Stage 4, markdown."]], ["multiple-timeframe-framework", ["Use multiple timeframes, each with a job. The higher timeframe answers which direction you're allowed to trade: weekly for a swing trader, daily or hourly for an intraday trader. The middle timeframe answers whether there's a setup, like a pullback or pattern at a level: daily for swing, fifteen minutes for intraday. The lower timeframe answers exactly where to enter and put the stop: four hour or hourly for swing, five or one minute for intraday. Keep a ratio of roughly four to six times between timeframes. Trade in the direction of the higher one, and remember that a level visible on several timeframes is stronger.", "The takeaway: The higher timeframe allows a direction, the middle finds the setup, the lower times the trigger.", "Pause and answer: Swing trader's trend timeframe?", "⏸", "Answer: Weekly."]], ["trend-vs-range-what-changes", ["Trends and ranges need opposite tactics. In a range, there's no clean sequence of higher highs or lower lows, just flat boundaries; you fade the edges or wait for a breakout, oscillators like RSI work, the main risk is a breakout stopping out your fades, and volatility contracts. In a trend, there's a clear sequence of higher highs and higher lows, or the opposite; you buy pullbacks or sell rallies, moving averages and ADX work, the main risk is entering late or false breakouts, and volatility expands on the impulse legs. Using trend tools in a range, or range tools in a trend, is the most common way indicators fail.", "The takeaway: Ranges call for fading edges and oscillators; trends for buying pullbacks, breaks of structure and trend tools.", "Pause and answer: Which indicators suit ranges?", "⏸", "Answer: Oscillators such as RSI and stochastics."]], ["trend-or-range-a-quick-test", ["A quick test for your trading timeframe. Is there a clear sequence of higher highs and higher lows, or lower highs and lower lows? Then it's a trend: trade pullbacks and breaks of structure, and trail your stops. If not, is price bouncing between flat levels? Then it's a range: fade the edges with tight stops. If not, are the bands or the average true range contracting? Then it's a squeeze: wait for a breakout with volume. And if none of these, it's chop: stand aside or cut your size.", "The takeaway: Clean structure means trend; flat levels mean range; contracting bands mean a squeeze.", "Pause and answer: Bands and ATR contracting with no structure: what then?", "⏸", "Answer: A squeeze: wait for a breakout."]]], "price": [[null, ["Price action is the raw material. Indicators are derived from it, so learn to read it directly."]], ["support-and-resistance", ["Support is where buying has appeared before: prior lows, round numbers and high volume areas. Resistance is where selling has appeared. Why do levels work? Market memory, unfilled orders left behind, and many traders watching the same obvious numbers. A level is stronger when it produced a sharp reversal, on high volume, on several timeframes, and recently. But be careful: many tests can also exhaust a level, as the orders defending it get used up. Role reversal is important: broken resistance tends to become support, and broken support becomes resistance. And always treat levels as zones, not exact prices.", "The takeaway: Levels work through memory, unfilled orders and attention; strength comes from sharp reversals, touches and volume.", "Pause and answer: Why do levels work?", "⏸", "Answer: Memory, unfilled orders left behind, and attention on obvious numbers."]], ["trendlines-and-channels", ["An uptrend line joins higher lows; a downtrend line joins lower highs. Two touches draw it, and a third validates it. Don't force lines through every wick, and remember steeper lines break sooner. A channel adds a parallel line through the opposite swings, so you can buy the lower rail and sell the upper, or trade a confirmed break. A trendline break means the trend is weakening, not necessarily reversing. And moving averages, like the twenty, fifty and two hundred period ones, plus VWAP, act as dynamic support in uptrends.", "The takeaway: Two touches draw a line, a third validates it; steeper lines break sooner.", "Pause and answer: What does an uptrend line connect?", "⏸", "Answer: Higher lows."]], ["market-structure-vocabulary", ["Market structure vocabulary. Higher highs and higher lows define an uptrend, so buy pullbacks to the higher low. Lower highs and lower lows define a downtrend, so sell rallies to the lower high. A break of structure is a close beyond the last higher high, or lower low, confirming the trend continues. A market structure shift, also called a change of character, is the first lower low in an uptrend, or the first higher high in a downtrend; it's an early warning of a reversal or a deeper pullback. And a swing point is a pivot with lower highs on both sides, or higher lows; define it consistently, like two or three bars on each side.", "The takeaway: HH/HL is an uptrend, LH/LL a downtrend; BOS continues the trend, CHoCH warns of a change.", "Pause and answer: What's a break of structure (BOS)?", "⏸", "Answer: A close beyond the last higher high (or lower low)."]], ["liquidity-grabs-and-stop-hunts", ["Stops cluster just beyond obvious highs, lows and round numbers. Price often sweeps through those levels, triggers the stops, then reverses, leaving a long wick. That gives you a setup: a sweep below support, a strong close back inside, then a long trade with the stop below the wick. It also gives you a defence: put your stop beyond the obvious level with a buffer, or use a close beyond the level as your exit. And a mindset point: most of what traders call \"manipulation\" is ordinary liquidity mechanics. Plan for it instead of blaming it.", "The takeaway: Price often sweeps stop clusters beyond obvious levels, then reverses, leaving a long wick.", "Pause and answer: Where do stops cluster?", "⏸", "Answer: Just beyond obvious highs, lows and round numbers."]], ["price-action-entries-and-exits", ["Price action entries and exits. Long at support: wait for a bullish candle at the level, enter above its high, put the stop below the level with a buffer, and target the next resistance or twice your risk. Short at resistance is the mirror image. Breakouts: enter on a close beyond the level or on the retest, with the stop back inside the range, targeting the range height projected from the breakout. Trend pullbacks: enter on a rejection at the higher low, trendline or moving average, with the stop below the higher low, targeting the prior high, then trail. After a bearish structure shift, short the retest of the broken higher low from below. And after a liquidity sweep, enter on the close back inside, with the stop beyond the wick, targeting the opposite side of the range.", "The takeaway: Enter on a confirming candle at the level, stop beyond it with a buffer, target the next level or 1:2.", "Pause and answer: Long at support: where's the entry?", "⏸", "Answer: Over the high of a bullish candle at the level."]]], "candles": [[null, ["Candlesticks come from Japanese rice traders, and Steve Nison brought them to the West. They're a compact picture of the battle between buyers and sellers in each period."]], ["candle-anatomy-and-psychology", ["Candle anatomy. The body runs from open to close, green when the close is above the open, and its size shows conviction. The upper wick shows sellers rejecting higher prices; the lower wick shows buyers rejecting lower prices. A close near the high means buyers were in control; near the low, sellers were. A small body means indecision, and that only matters after a strong move or at a level. The close location value captures this in one number: the close minus the low, divided by the whole range. One means it closed on the high, zero on the low.", "The takeaway: Body shows conviction, upper wicks rejected highs, lower wicks rejected lows, and the close location tells who won.", "Pause and answer: Upper wick formula?", "⏸", "Answer: High − max(open, close)."]], ["context-beats-the-pattern", ["The most important candle lesson: context beats the pattern. A hammer at support or at a higher low means far more than one in the middle of a range. A reversal candle against the higher timeframe trend is lower odds. Above average volume on the signal candle adds conviction. Sequence matters: a doji after three large down candles at support is exhaustion, but after one candle it's noise. And wait for confirmation, meaning the next candle closes in the signal's direction before you enter.", "The takeaway: Level, trend, volume and sequence decide whether a candle pattern means anything.", "Pause and answer: A hammer mid-range versus at support: which matters?", "⏸", "Answer: At support (or a higher low)."]], ["single-candle-patterns", ["Single candle patterns. A doji has an open roughly equal to the close; it means indecision, a reversal only if confirmed. A hammer has a small body at the top and a lower wick at least twice the body, after a decline at support; it's bullish, so buy over its high with the stop under its low. A hanging man is the same shape after a rally, at resistance, a bearish warning. An inverted hammer, with a long upper wick after a decline, is bullish if confirmed. A shooting star is that same shape after a rally at resistance, and it's bearish. A marubozu is a full body with no wicks, showing momentum in the body's direction. And a spinning top, a small body with wicks on both sides, shows a loss of momentum but isn't a trigger on its own. Notice that the same shape means different things depending on where it appears.", "The takeaway: Doji, hammer, shooting star and marubozu: trade the next candle's break with the stop beyond the pattern.", "Pause and answer: Is a doji a reversal signal on its own?", "⏸", "Answer: No; indecision, a reversal only if confirmed."]], ["two-candle-patterns", ["Two candle patterns. A bullish engulfing candle's body completely covers the previous bearish body; bearish engulfing is the opposite. A piercing line gaps down and closes above the midpoint of the previous red body, bullish but weaker than engulfing; dark cloud cover is its bearish twin. Tweezer tops and bottoms are two candles with matching highs or lows at a level. A harami is a small body inside the previous large body, showing the trend pausing; a harami cross, where the second candle is a doji, is stronger. And counterattack lines are opposite coloured candles closing at the same price, a weaker reversal signal.", "The takeaway: Engulfing, piercing, dark cloud, harami and tweezers: a second candle that overrides the first.", "Pause and answer: Define a bullish engulfing.", "⏸", "Answer: A bullish body fully engulfing the prior bearish body."]], ["three-candle-patterns", ["Three candle patterns. A morning star is a large red candle, a small body that gaps down, then a large green candle closing past the midpoint of the first; it's bullish. The evening star is the bearish mirror. An abandoned baby is a star that's a doji gapped away on both sides: rare, and a strong reversal. Three white soldiers are three green candles, each opening inside the previous body and closing near its high; three black crows are the bearish version. An inside bar sits entirely within the previous mother bar's range, a coil you trade on the break of the mother bar. And an outside bar's range engulfs the previous bar, and you follow the direction of its close.", "The takeaway: Morning and evening stars, abandoned babies, three soldiers and crows add confirmation across three bars.", "Pause and answer: Morning star sequence?", "⏸", "Answer: Large red, small-bodied star, then large green closing past candle 1's midpoint."]], ["nison-s-extra-patterns", ["A few more from Nison. A belt hold opens at its extreme and runs the other way; stronger the longer its body. Upside gap two crows: after a rally, a small red candle gaps up, and a second red engulfs it but stays above the first candle; bearish. Tasuki gaps and rising or falling three methods are continuation patterns, where a brief counter move fails to undo the trend. The doji family: long legged, with big wicks both sides; gravestone, with open, close and low equal, bearish at tops; dragonfly, with open, close and high equal, bullish at bottoms; and four price, with no range at all, a sign of illiquidity. And Nison's own rule: candles give early warnings, while Western tools give targets. So pair every candle with a trendline, moving average or level.", "The takeaway: Belt-holds, upside-gap two crows and other Nison patterns refine the main set.", "Pause and answer: What is a bullish belt-hold?", "⏸", "Answer: A candle opening on its low and running up."]], ["should-i-trade-this-candle", ["Here's a four question filter for any candle signal. One: is it at a level you marked before the candle formed? If not, skip it; a pattern in the middle of a range is noise. Two: does it agree with the higher timeframe trend? If not, it's counter trend, so use half size and wait for a confirming close. Three: is the volume or range above average? If not, wait for the next candle to confirm. Four: with the stop beyond the candle, does the trade still offer at least one to two reward to risk? If yes, trade it, entering beyond the candle's extreme. If not, pass, because the reward doesn't pay for the risk.", "The takeaway: Trade candles only at pre-marked levels, with the higher-timeframe trend, with a defined stop.", "Pause and answer: A pattern in the middle of a range: trade it?", "⏸", "Answer: Skip it: noise."]]], "patterns": [[null, ["Chart patterns are larger shapes that form over weeks or months. They're useful because they give you three things at once: a reason to enter, a place to put your stop, and a target."]], ["reversal-patterns", ["Reversal patterns. The head and shoulders top has three peaks with the middle one highest, and a neckline through the two lows between them. It confirms on a close below the neckline; the target is the height from head to neckline, projected down; the stop goes above the right shoulder. The volume tell: heavier on the left shoulder, light on the right, expanding on the break. The inverse head and shoulders is the bullish mirror. A double top is an M shape that confirms on a close below the middle low, usually with lower volume on the second peak; a double bottom is a W that confirms above the middle high. Triple tops and bottoms are three tests of one level. A rounding bottom, or saucer, is a slow U shaped turn. A rising wedge has two converging lines both rising, and breaks down; a falling wedge has both falling, and breaks up. For wedges, the target is back to where the wedge started.", "The takeaway: Head and shoulders, double and triple tops and bottoms confirm on a neckline close, with height-based targets.", "Pause and answer: Head and shoulders target?", "⏸", "Answer: Head-to-neckline height projected down from the neckline."]], ["continuation-patterns", ["Continuation patterns pause a trend before it resumes. A bull or bear flag is a sharp pole followed by a small channel sloping against it; trade the break in the pole's direction, targeting the pole's length. Flags lasting longer than about three weeks tend to underperform. A pennant is a pole followed by a small symmetrical triangle, with volume drying up inside. An ascending triangle has a flat top and rising lows, showing buyers getting more aggressive each test. A descending triangle has a flat bottom and falling highs, with a bearish bias, though it can still break up. A symmetrical triangle can break either way, usually with the prior trend, and the best breakouts come two thirds to three quarters of the way to the apex. A rectangle has flat support and resistance: fade the edges inside, trade the break outside. And William O'Neil's cup and handle: a U shaped cup of seven to sixty five weeks, twelve to thirty three percent deep, with a shallow handle of one to two weeks in its upper half. Buy the break above the handle.", "The takeaway: Flags, pennants, triangles and rectangles usually continue the prior move; targets use the pole or height.", "Pause and answer: Flag target?", "⏸", "Answer: Pole length from the breakout."]], ["breakout-confirmation", ["How to confirm a breakout. Look for a close beyond the level, not just a wick, because wicks are often stop runs. Volume should be above average; one and a half times the fifty day average is a common bar. A retest, where broken resistance holds as support, gives a tighter entry. A daily close outranks a five minute break. And the invalidation: a close back inside the pattern means the breakout failed, so exit or even reverse.", "The takeaway: Close beyond the level, volume at least 1.5× average, and ideally a retest that holds.", "Pause and answer: Is a wick beyond resistance a breakout?", "⏸", "Answer: No; wait for a close beyond it."]], ["false-breakouts", ["False breakouts happen because stops cluster beyond obvious levels, because there's no follow through, or because the break showed only on a noisy timeframe. Defend against them by waiting for the close, placing the stop beyond the level plus a buffer, and entering half size, adding on the retest. You can also exploit them: a failed break that closes back inside often runs all the way to the other side of the range. Wyckoff called these springs and upthrusts. And Thomas Bulkowski found that about two in three breakouts throw back to the breakout level, so expect a retest rather than panicking.", "The takeaway: Stops beyond obvious levels and no follow-through create fakeouts; wait for closes, buffer stops, add on the retest.", "Pause and answer: How do you defend against false breakouts?", "⏸", "Answer: Wait for the close, buffer the stop, enter half size and add on the retest."]], ["pattern-statistics-bulkowski-us-stocks", ["These statistics come from Bulkowski's study of US stocks, and they're useful to set expectations. In bull markets, the cup with handle averaged around a fifty four percent move with only five percent failures. Rounding bottoms were the best performer in bear markets. Head and shoulders bottoms were one of the most reliable reversals. Some surprises: rising wedges fail about half the time in bull markets, so take profits fast. Flags average only about a nine percent move and hit their target less than half the time, so treat them as short term trades. And around half of double bottom candidates never confirm, which is why you wait for the breakout. Most breakouts throw back or pull back to the breakout level. Remember these are historical averages across many charts, not a promise for the one in front of you.", "The takeaway: Bulkowski's US data ranks patterns: cup with handle and rounding bottoms rate highly, but throwbacks are common.", "Pause and answer: Average move of a cup with handle in bull markets?", "⏸", "Answer: About +54%."]], ["gaps", ["Gaps come in types. Common gaps form inside a range on low volume; they're noise, and most fill within a week. Breakaway gaps launch out of a base or pattern on high volume; they mark a new trend and rarely fill soon. Runaway, or measuring, gaps appear mid trend and often mark roughly the halfway point; they may take months to fill. Exhaustion gaps come late in a trend with climactic volume, a last burst before reversal, and most close within a week. And an island reversal is a gap, a few bars, then an opposite gap, trapping traders and producing a strong reversal.", "The takeaway: Common gaps fill fast, breakaway gaps rarely close soon, runaway gaps mark mid-trend, exhaustion gaps end moves.", "Pause and answer: How often do common gaps close within a week?", "⏸", "Answer: About 85–92%."]], ["measuring-targets", ["Measuring targets. For a head and shoulders, take the head to neckline height and project it from the neckline. For a flag, add the flagpole length to the breakout. For a triangle, add the widest height. A practical tip: half height targets are hit far more often than full height ones. For Bulkowski's big W, the full target was hit seventy four percent of the time in bull markets, but the half target eighty eight percent. So take partial profits at half height and trail the rest. And remember the next higher timeframe level often stops price before any measured target.", "The takeaway: Targets project the pattern's height; half-height targets are hit far more often than full height.", "Pause and answer: Triangle target?", "⏸", "Answer: Breakout ± the widest height."]]], "indicators": [[null, ["Indicators are formulas applied to price and volume. They don't contain new information, but they summarise it in useful ways. The skill is knowing which question each one answers."]], ["indicator-quick-reference", ["This big table is the reference, so let me group it by family. Trend tools: simple and exponential moving averages, usually twenty, fifty and two hundred periods; price above a rising average is bullish. VWAP, the volume weighted average price, is the intraday institutional benchmark and resets each day. ADX measures trend strength regardless of direction: above twenty five means trending, below twenty means ranging. Ichimoku is a complete trend system where price above the cloud is bullish. Parabolic SAR and Supertrend are trailing stop tools that flip sides when the trend changes. Momentum tools: RSI, on a zero to hundred scale, with seventy and thirty as extremes in ranges. MACD, the difference of the twelve and twenty six period averages, with a nine period signal line. Stochastic, which compares the close to the recent range. CCI and rate of change. Volatility tools: Bollinger bands, two standard deviations around a twenty period average, where a squeeze precedes expansion. ATR, the average true range, for stops and sizing. Keltner channels, built from ATR, and Donchian channels, the highest high and lowest low of N bars, used by the famous Turtle traders. Volume tools: on balance volume and the accumulation distribution line, read for divergences; money flow index, an RSI that includes volume; volume profile, showing volume at each price; and anchored VWAP, the average cost of everyone who traded since an event. Each tool also has a failure mode, listed in the last column, so read that column carefully.", "The takeaway: Know each indicator's family, default, formula, read and failure mode.", "Pause and answer: EMA smoothing factor?", "⏸", "Answer: α = 2 ÷ (N + 1)."]], ["which-indicator-for-which-job", ["Pick indicators by the question you need answered. Trend direction? Moving averages or the Ichimoku cloud. Whether there's a trend at all? ADX. Whether a move is stretched or tiring? Oscillators like RSI and stochastic, and their divergences. A stop distance and position size? ATR, with the stop one and a half to three ATRs away. Whether real money is behind a move? Volume tools: volume versus its average, OBV, VWAP and the profile. And if none of these, go back to price action, because levels and structure beat any indicator.", "The takeaway: Direction → moving averages; trend or not → ADX; momentum → RSI or MACD; volatility → ATR or bands; conviction → volume.", "Pause and answer: Which indicator says whether there's a trend at all?", "⏸", "Answer: ADX: above 25 trending, below 20 ranging."]], ["divergences", ["Divergences compare price with an oscillator. A regular bullish divergence: price makes a lower low but the oscillator makes a higher low, so selling momentum is fading and a reversal up may come. Regular bearish: price makes a higher high, the oscillator a lower high. Hidden divergences signal continuation: a hidden bullish divergence is a higher low in price with a lower low in the oscillator, a pullback to buy in an uptrend. Two warnings: a divergence is a warning, not a trigger, so wait for a structure or trendline break. And strong trends can print several divergences before they actually turn.", "The takeaway: Regular divergence warns of reversal; hidden divergence signals trend continuation.", "Pause and answer: Regular bullish divergence?", "⏸", "Answer: Price makes a lower low while the oscillator makes a higher low."]], ["moving-average-playbook", ["The moving average playbook. As a trend filter, take longs only above a rising two hundred day average, the most watched line for stocks and indices. For pullback zones, use the twenty period average in strong trends and the fifty in normal ones. The golden cross, the fifty crossing above the two hundred, and the death cross are slow regime markers, not entries. Price above the twenty, above the fifty, above the two hundred, all rising, is the textbook stage two picture. And exponential averages react faster but whipsaw more, while simple averages are slower, though the fifty and two hundred day simple averages are the market convention.", "The takeaway: Trade longs above a rising 200-day SMA, buy pullbacks to the 20 or 50 EMA, and treat crosses as slow regime markers.", "Pause and answer: Pullback zone in a strong trend?", "⏸", "Answer: The 20 EMA."]], ["rsi-in-context", ["RSI needs context. In a range, it oscillates between thirty and seventy, and you fade the extremes at the range edges. In a bull market, it tends to hold between forty and eighty, so dips to forty or fifty are buyable pullbacks. In a bear market, it holds between twenty and sixty, and rallies to fifty five or sixty tend to fail. A failure swing is when RSI breaks its own prior swing before price does. And Larry Connors popularised a two period RSI: a reading below ten in an uptrend is a classic short term mean reversion entry. The big lesson: selling just because RSI says overbought in a strong trend is a classic beginner mistake.", "The takeaway: RSI ranges shift with regime: 30–70 in ranges, 40–80 in bull trends, 20–60 in bear trends.", "Pause and answer: In a bull regime, where are RSI dips buyable?", "⏸", "Answer: Around 40–50."]], ["volatility-toolkit", ["The volatility toolkit. True range is the largest of three things: today's high minus low, the gap from yesterday's close to today's high, or to today's low. ATR is a smoothed average of it. Percent B tells you where price sits within the Bollinger bands, and bandwidth measures how wide they are. A squeeze, meaning bandwidth at a six month low or Bollinger bands inside the Keltner channels, warns that a big move is coming, but not its direction. The chandelier exit trails a stop three ATRs below the highest high since entry. And Mark Minervini's volatility contraction pattern shows successive pullbacks shrinking, say twenty five, then fifteen, then eight percent, before a breakout.", "The takeaway: True range, ATR, %B and bandwidth; a squeeze means a big move is coming, direction unknown.", "Pause and answer: True range formula?", "⏸", "Answer: max(H − L, |H − prev C|, |L − prev C|)."]], ["volume-rules", ["Volume rules. Volume confirms price: rising volume on up moves and falling volume on pullbacks means a healthy trend. Breakouts need volume, at least one and a half times average; a breakout on light volume is suspect. Climax volume after a long run often marks exhaustion. On a volume profile, price moves fast through low volume areas and stalls at high volume ones, and the point of control, the price with the most volume, acts like a magnet. And relative volume, today compared with the same time on recent days, finds the stocks that are in play intraday.", "The takeaway: Volume confirms healthy trends, breakouts need it, and climax volume after a long run often marks exhaustion.", "Pause and answer: Healthy uptrend volume pattern?", "⏸", "Answer: Rising on up moves, falling on pullbacks."]], ["common-indicator-mistakes", ["The common mistakes. Stacking three momentum oscillators, which is the same information three times. Selling \"overbought\" in a strong trend. Tuning parameters until the backtest shines, which is curve fitting. Ignoring the regime: trend tools in ranges and oscillators in trends. And treating crossovers as entries without a level, stop or size. The rule of thumb: one trend tool, plus one momentum or volume tool, plus ATR, is enough.", "The takeaway: Stacking oscillators, selling overbought in trends, curve-fitting, and ignoring the regime.", "Pause and answer: Why is stacking three momentum oscillators a mistake?", "⏸", "Answer: It's the same information three times."]], ["indicators-in-python-pandas", ["This card has the indicators in Python with pandas. The exponential average uses pandas' exponentially weighted mean with a span of N. RSI splits each day's change into gains and losses, smooths each with Wilder's method, which is just an exponential average with alpha equal to one over N, and computes a hundred minus a hundred divided by one plus the ratio. MACD is the fast average minus the slow one, its signal line, and the difference between them as a histogram. ATR takes the maximum of the three true range candidates and smooths it. Bollinger uses a rolling mean and the population standard deviation, the way charting tools do. And VWAP, for one session of intraday bars, is the running sum of typical price times volume, divided by the running sum of volume. These were checked against a standard library: MACD, Bollinger and VWAP match exactly, and RSI and ATR match after the warm up bars.", "The takeaway: EMA via ewm(span=n), RSI with Wilder smoothing via ewm(alpha=1/n).", "Pause and answer: pandas EMA one-liner?", "⏸", "Answer: s.ewm(span=n, adjust=False).mean()."]]], "fib": [[null, ["This section covers Fibonacci levels, Elliott waves, point and figure charts and other classic tools. Treat them as maps, not as laws."]], ["fibonacci-retracements-and-extensions", ["Fibonacci retracements measure how deep a pullback is, as a fraction of the prior swing. Twenty three point six percent is a shallow pullback in a very strong trend. Thirty eight point two is a normal pullback in a strong trend. Fifty percent isn't actually a Fibonacci ratio, but it's widely watched, going back to Dow's half retracement. Sixty one point eight is a deep pullback, often the last line for the trend to stay intact. Seventy eight point six is very deep. And extensions of one hundred and twenty seven and one hundred and sixty one point eight percent give targets beyond the prior swing. In an uptrend, draw from the swing low to the swing high. Fibonacci levels work best where they coincide with prior support, a moving average or a trendline. That overlap is called confluence.", "The takeaway: 38.2% for strong trends, 61.8% as the last line, 50% widely watched though not Fibonacci.", "Pause and answer: Which retracement suits a very strong trend?", "⏸", "Answer: 23.6% to 38.2%."]], ["elliott-wave-basics", ["Elliott wave theory says trends move in five waves and correct in three. The impulse goes one, two, three, four, five with the trend, then a correction A, B, C. There are three hard rules. Wave two never retraces beyond the start of wave one. Wave three is never the shortest. And wave four doesn't overlap wave one's price territory. Some guidelines: wave three is often one point six one eight times wave one, waves two and four alternate between sharp and sideways, and wave five often equals wave one. Wave two typically retraces fifty to sixty two percent, and wave four about thirty eight. Elliott counts are subjective, so use the theory as a map of where you might be in a trend, and trade the invalidation levels the hard rules give you.", "The takeaway: Five impulse waves, three corrective; wave 2 never passes wave 1's start, wave 3 is never shortest, wave 4 doesn't overlap wave 1.", "Pause and answer: Name the three hard rules.", "⏸", "Answer: Wave 2 never retraces beyond wave 1's start; wave 3 is never the shortest; wave 4 doesn't overlap wave 1."]], ["elliott-wave-cycle", ["This diagram walks through the psychology of each wave. Wave one: early buyers. Wave two: a deep retrace of fifty to sixty two percent that shakes them out. Wave three: the longest and strongest, when everyone notices. Wave four: shallow and sideways. Wave five: the final push, often with a momentum divergence. Then wave A, the first drop; wave B, a bull trap that fools people into thinking the uptrend is back; and wave C, which completes the correction.", "The takeaway: Wave 3 is the longest and strongest; wave 5 is the final push, often with divergence.", "Pause and answer: Typical retrace of wave 2?", "⏸", "Answer: 50–62%."]], ["point-and-figure-charts", ["Point and figure charts plot only price, ignoring time. Columns of X's show rises, and columns of O's show falls. The box size sets the smallest move counted, like one rupee for a hundred rupee stock. With the common three box reversal, a new column starts only after price reverses by three boxes, which filters out noise. A double top buy signal is when an X column rises above the previous X column; a double bottom sell is when an O column falls below the previous O column. The count method estimates targets from the width of a congestion area. They're great for clean support, resistance and trendlines without noise.", "The takeaway: Point and figure plots only price in X and O columns, ignoring time; box size and a 3-box reversal set sensitivity.", "Pause and answer: What does P&F ignore?", "⏸", "Answer: Time and small moves."]], ["futures-specific-signals-murphy", ["John Murphy's futures signals combine price, volume and open interest, the number of open contracts. Price up with volume and open interest up is strong: new longs are entering. Price up with both falling is weak: it's a short covering rally. Price down with both rising is strong on the bearish side: new shorts are entering. And price down with both falling is weak: long liquidation, possibly near a low. In the US, the Commitment of Traders report shows commercial hedgers, who are often right at extremes. In India, NSE publishes F and O open interest daily, including the split between foreign institutions, domestic institutions, proprietary traders and clients.", "The takeaway: Price, volume and open interest together separate strong new positioning from weak covering or liquidation.", "Pause and answer: Price up, volume and OI down?", "⏸", "Answer: Weak: a short-covering rally."]], ["other-classic-tools", ["Other classic tools. Pivot points are intraday levels computed from yesterday's high, low and close: the pivot is their average, and resistance and support levels are derived from it. Heikin Ashi candles average prices to smooth trends, but they aren't real prices, so never place orders from them. Renko charts use fixed size bricks and ignore time, giving clean trends but late signals. Seasonality, like \"sell in May\" or Diwali and budget effects in India, is weak alone but useful as a tie breaker. And market profile shows the time spent at each price, giving a value area and the initial balance.", "The takeaway: Pivot points, Heikin-Ashi, Renko and others; Heikin-Ashi candles aren't real prices.", "Pause and answer: Pivot point formula?", "⏸", "Answer: P = (H + L + C) ÷ 3."]]], "advanced": [[null, ["Now the advanced topics: smart money concepts, Wyckoff, intermarket analysis, breadth and sentiment."]], ["smart-money-concepts-smc", ["Smart money concepts have become popular, especially on social media. An order block is the last opposite candle before a strong impulsive move; you enter on a retest of that candle's range. A fair value gap is a three candle imbalance, where the ranges of the first and third candles don't overlap; price often returns to fill it. A liquidity pool is a set of equal highs or lows where stops cluster; you wait for the sweep and a close back inside. Break of structure and change of character, which we've met already. And premium and discount: above or below the fifty percent level of the current swing, so you buy in discount and sell in premium. My honest view: SMC mostly renames classic ideas like supply and demand zones, gaps and stop runs. Judge it by the same rules as anything else: level, confirmation, stop and size.", "The takeaway: Order blocks, fair value gaps and liquidity sweeps describe where large players left footprints; trade their retests with stops beyond.", "Pause and answer: What is an order block?", "⏸", "Answer: The last opposite candle before a strong impulsive move."]], ["wyckoff-accumulation-sequence", ["Richard Wyckoff described how large operators accumulate shares in a range. The sequence: preliminary support, then a selling climax where panic selling meets big buyers, an automatic rally, a secondary test of the lows, then the spring, a false break below the range that shakes out the last sellers. After that, a sign of strength, the last point of support, and finally the markup. Distribution at tops mirrors it: preliminary supply, a buying climax, an automatic reaction, a secondary test, the upthrust, a sign of weakness, the last point of supply, and markdown.", "The takeaway: Preliminary support, selling climax, automatic rally, secondary test, spring, sign of strength, last point of support, markup.", "Pause and answer: What is the spring in Wyckoff accumulation?", "⏸", "Answer: A shakeout below the range that quickly reverses."]], ["wyckoff-in-practice", ["Wyckoff in practice rests on three laws. Supply and demand drives price. Cause and effect: the longer the range, the bigger the eventual move. And effort versus result: heavy volume with little price progress means the other side is absorbing it. The range has phases A to E: A stops the prior trend, B builds the cause, C tests with a spring or upthrust, D shows strength or weakness, and E is the trend out of the range. The spring trade: go long after the false break below the range closes back inside, with the stop under the spring low, targeting the range top and then the measured move. The upthrust trade is its mirror image at the top of a distribution range.", "The takeaway: Three laws: supply and demand, cause and effect, effort versus result; phases A to E.", "Pause and answer: Wyckoff's cause and effect law?", "⏸", "Answer: The length of the range (cause) sets the size of the move (effect)."]], ["intermarket-relationships", ["Intermarket relationships connect markets. Rising bond yields compress stock valuations, especially for growth stocks. The US dollar and commodities usually move inversely, so a gold or oil breakout fighting a rising dollar is weaker. Gold moves inversely to real yields. Copper rises with economic growth, earning the nickname \"Doctor Copper\", so stocks rising while copper falls is a warning. Widening high yield credit spreads often lead stock market corrections. An inverted yield curve, with two year yields above ten year yields, has preceded recessions by six to twenty four months, and the re steepening after inversion is the late, risky stage. For India, watch the ten year government bond yield, the rupee against the dollar, crude oil, since India imports most of its oil, and foreign institutional flows, alongside the Nifty.", "The takeaway: Higher yields compress valuations, a strong dollar weighs on commodities; intermarket breaks give early warnings.", "Pause and answer: Bond yields breaking out: good or bad for long-duration growth stocks?", "⏸", "Answer: Bad: higher yields compress P/E."]], ["breadth-indicators", ["Breadth tells you how many stocks are participating in a move. The advance decline line is the running total of advancing minus declining stocks; if the index makes a new high but the line doesn't, the rally is narrow and fragile. The McClellan oscillator is the difference between two smoothed averages of net advances, and extreme readings mean breadth is stretched. New highs minus new lows: shrinking new highs in a rising market signal distribution. The percentage of stocks above their two hundred day average: above seventy percent is strong, below twenty or thirty is washed out. And the Arms index, or TRIN, compares advancing and declining volume; very high readings appear near capitulation.", "The takeaway: Breadth checks how many stocks join a move; an index high without an A/D high is a narrow, fragile rally.", "Pause and answer: Advance–decline line formula?", "⏸", "Answer: Cumulative (advances − declines)."]], ["sentiment-contrarian-at-extremes", ["Sentiment works as a contrarian indicator, but only at extremes. A very high put call ratio signals fear, which is contrarian bullish; very low signals complacency. Volatility index spikes, like VIX or India VIX, mark panic lows more reliably than tops. Extreme survey readings are contrarian. Clusters of insider buying are more informative than insider selling. Margin debt and IPO frenzies rise into tops. And from O'Neil: a follow through day, a big index gain on higher volume, usually four to ten days after a low, confirms a new uptrend, while four or five distribution days, the index falling on higher volume within a few weeks, warn of a top.", "The takeaway: Sentiment works contrarian at extremes: very high put/call or VIX spikes mark fear lows.", "Pause and answer: Very high put/call ratio: contrarian read?", "⏸", "Answer: Fear, which is contrarian bullish."]], ["relative-strength", ["Relative strength compares a stock with the index: divide the stock's price by the index. A rising line means the stock is beating the market, even if both are falling. A relative strength line making a new high before price does is one of the strongest leadership signs. The Mansfield version compares the ratio with its own fifty two week average, so above zero means outperforming. An RS rating ranks a stock's twelve month performance from one to ninety nine, and leaders usually rate eighty or ninety plus. Relative rotation graphs place sectors in leading, weakening, lagging and improving quadrants. And the top down idea: a strong market, then the strongest sector, then the strongest stocks in that sector.", "The takeaway: RS line = stock ÷ index; an RS high before a price high is a strong leadership tell.", "Pause and answer: What does a rising RS line in a falling market mean?", "⏸", "Answer: The stock is beating the market."]], ["india-market-data-to-watch", ["India specific data to watch. India VIX: rising sharply means fear, low and falling means a calm trend. The Nifty option chain: big put open interest often acts as support and big call open interest as resistance near expiry. Daily foreign and domestic institutional cash flows: persistent FII selling with DII buying is a tug of war. Participant wise F and O open interest: the long to short ratio of FII index futures is a positioning gauge. Delivery percentage: high delivery on up days means genuine buying rather than intraday churn. And the F and O ban list: no fresh positions are allowed, so expect erratic moves.", "The takeaway: India VIX, option-chain PCR and max OI strikes, FII/DII flows and breadth round out the Indian picture.", "Pause and answer: Near expiry, what does big put OI at a strike often act as?", "⏸", "Answer: Support."]]], "strategies": [[null, ["Now we turn analysis into trading. A strategy is a combination of a market regime, a setup, an entry trigger and an exit rule."]], ["strategy-styles-compared", ["Strategy styles compared. Scalping holds for seconds or minutes, needs sixty percent winners or more because its reward is at most equal to its risk, and costs and slippage are its main enemy. Intraday momentum holds minutes to hours, wins about half the time at one and a half to two times reward to risk, and works best on stocks in play and news days. Swing trading holds days to weeks on daily and four hour charts, winning forty to fifty five percent at two to three times reward, with overnight gaps as the main risk. Position trading holds weeks to months, winning only thirty five to fifty percent but with rewards of three to five times risk or more. Trend following wins only thirty five to forty five percent of the time but its winners are two to five times its losers, and it suffers long drawdowns in choppy markets. Mean reversion wins fifty five to seventy percent with smaller rewards, works in ranges, and gets hurt in strong trends. And breakout trading works after contraction, with false breakouts as its risk. Notice the pattern: a high win rate always comes with small wins, and big wins always come with a low win rate. There's no free lunch.", "The takeaway: Scalping, intraday momentum, swing, position and trend following trade off win rate, reward to risk and costs.", "Pause and answer: Main risk of scalping?", "⏸", "Answer: Costs and slippage eating the edge."]], ["setup-library", ["The setup library. Trend pullback: in an uptrend above a rising fifty day average, buy over a bullish candle at the twenty or fifty period average or the prior higher low, with the stop below the pullback low, and trail. Base breakout: after a base of five weeks or more with tightening and drying volume, buy a close above the pivot on one and a half times volume, with a stop below the base or seven to eight percent, targeting twenty to twenty five percent. The spring, or failed breakdown: a sweep below support that closes back inside. The range fade: with ADX below twenty, a rejection candle at a tested edge, targeting the midpoint. The opening range breakout: for a stock gapping on news, the break of the first fifteen or thirty minute range. VWAP reclaim: in an intraday uptrend that dipped, a close back above VWAP on volume. RSI two pullback: above the two hundred day average, when the two period RSI is below ten, exiting on a close above the five day average. And the Turtle style Donchian breakout: a new twenty or fifty five day high, with a stop two ATRs away.", "The takeaway: Each setup has context, trigger, stop and exit: trend pullbacks, base breakouts, range fades and more.", "Pause and answer: Trend pullback trigger?", "⏸", "Answer: A bullish candle at the 20/50 EMA or prior higher low; buy over its high."]], ["which-strategy-fits-right-now", ["Which strategy fits right now? Read the regime on your timeframe. A clear trend with ADX above twenty five? Trend following: pullbacks and breaks of structure, with trailing stops. A clean range with tested edges? Mean reversion: fade the edges and target the midpoint. Volatility contracting, a squeeze or inside bars? Breakout: wait for a close beyond the range on volume. A catalyst with heavy volume, like an earnings gap? Momentum: the first pullback or the opening range break. And if none of these: no trade. Cash is a position.", "The takeaway: Trend with ADX above 25 → trend following; clean range → mean reversion; squeeze → breakout; unclear → stand aside.", "Pause and answer: Clear HH/HL and ADX above 25: which strategy?", "⏸", "Answer: Trend following: pullbacks and break-of-structure entries."]], ["breakout-trade-plan", ["This diagram is a complete breakout plan. Start with a base of five weeks or more after a prior uptrend. Pullbacks contract and volume dries up. Mark the pivot, the top of the handle or base. Buy the close above the pivot on one and a half times average volume. Put the stop under the last contraction, at most seven to eight percent away. Add only if the stock holds the pivot on a retest. And sell into a twenty to twenty five percent gain, or trail the fifty day line.", "The takeaway: A 5+ week base after an uptrend, contracting pullbacks, a pivot, a close above it on 1.5× volume, stop below the pivot.", "Pause and answer: What volume confirms the breakout?", "⏸", "Answer: About 1.5× average."]], ["can-slim-o-neil", ["CAN SLIM is William O'Neil's growth stock method, and it blends fundamentals and technicals. C: current quarterly earnings per share up at least twenty to twenty five percent year on year, with sales growth too. A: annual earnings growth of at least twenty five percent over three to five years, with return on equity above seventeen percent. N: something new, a product, management or industry change, with the price at or near new highs out of a base. S: supply and demand, with big volume on up days. L: a leader, not a laggard, with a relative strength rating of eighty or more. I: rising institutional sponsorship. And M: market direction, buying only in a confirmed uptrend. The sell rules: cut every loss at seven to eight percent below the buy point, and take most gains at twenty to twenty five percent, with one exception. If a stock gains twenty percent within one to three weeks of breaking out, hold it for eight weeks, because that's a sign of a potential big winner.", "The takeaway: O'Neil's CAN SLIM combines earnings growth, new highs, supply and demand, leadership, institutional support and market direction.", "Pause and answer: What does the C stand for?", "⏸", "Answer: Current quarterly EPS up at least 20–25% year on year."]], ["pyramiding-and-scaling-schwager", ["Jack Schwager's rules for pyramiding, which means adding to a position. Add only to positions already showing a profit. Don't add if the new combined stop would turn the whole position into a loss. Each added unit is no larger than the first, so it's a pyramid, not an inverted one. Add at logical points, like a breakout from a consolidation or a retest. And never average down a losing trade. Scaling in is only for trades that are already working, with total risk fixed in advance.", "The takeaway: Add only to winners, never so the combined stop turns the position into a loss, and each add no larger than the first.", "Pause and answer: Should you add to a losing position?", "⏸", "Answer: No; add only to positions already in profit."]], ["exits-and-trailing-stops", ["Exits matter as much as entries. A fixed target, the next level or twice your risk, suits ranges and mean reversion. Partial plus trail: take half at one or two times risk, move the stop to breakeven, and trail the rest, which suits swing trades. A structure trail moves the stop below each new higher low, for trends. A moving average trail exits on a close below the twenty period or fifty day average, for momentum leaders. The chandelier exit trails three ATRs below the highest high. And a time stop exits if there's no progress after a set number of bars, for breakouts that stall.", "The takeaway: Fixed targets suit ranges; partial-plus-trail suits swings; structure and MA trails suit trends.", "Pause and answer: Partial-plus-trail method?", "⏸", "Answer: Take half at 1–2R, move the stop to breakeven, trail the rest."]]], "risk": [[null, ["This is the most important section on the sheet. Analysis decides whether you trade; risk management decides whether you survive."]], ["position-size-from-the-stop", ["Position size comes from the stop, never the other way round. The risk amount is your account times the risk percentage. The risk per share is the distance from entry to stop. Shares equal the risk amount divided by the risk per share, rounded down. Here's the worked example. An account of ten lakh rupees, risking one percent, is ten thousand rupees of risk. Entry at five hundred, stop at four eighty, is twenty rupees risk per share. So ten thousand divided by twenty is five hundred shares. The position is worth two and a half lakh rupees, a quarter of your capital, but only one percent of the account is at risk. With a target of five forty, you make forty per share against twenty of risk, a reward to risk of two. Always pick the stop first, then the size. For futures and options, risk per unit is the stop distance times the lot size.", "The takeaway: Shares = account × risk% ÷ |entry − stop|, rounded down.", "Pause and answer: ₹10 lakh account, 1% risk: rupee risk?", "⏸", "Answer: ₹10,000."]], ["win-rate-needed-to-break-even", ["How often do you need to win to break even? The formula is one divided by one plus your reward to risk ratio. At half a unit of reward per unit of risk, you need sixty seven percent winners. At one to one, fifty percent. At two to one, thirty three percent. At three to one, twenty five percent. Now add costs: at a tenth of your risk per trade in costs, a scalper aiming for half a unit of reward needs seventy three percent winners. That's why costs hurt small target styles the most.", "The takeaway: Break-even win rate = 1 ÷ (1 + R); costs push it higher.", "Pause and answer: Break-even win rate at 2:1?", "⏸", "Answer: 33.3% (about 36.7% with 0.1R costs)."]], ["expectancy-and-kelly", ["Expectancy is what you make per trade on average, in units of risk: your win rate times your average win, minus your loss rate times your average loss. A forty percent win rate with average wins of two units gives plus zero point two units per trade. Fifty percent with two unit wins gives plus half a unit. The Kelly criterion gives the theoretically optimal fraction of capital to risk, but full Kelly is far too aggressive for real, uncertain edges. Use a quarter to half Kelly as a ceiling, or simply risk a fixed half a percent to two percent per trade.", "The takeaway: Expectancy in R measures edge; Kelly f* = (p·b − q) ÷ b gives the growth-optimal fraction, usually bet at a fraction.", "Pause and answer: 40% wins at 2R: expectancy?", "⏸", "Answer: +0.20R."]], ["drawdowns-the-asymmetry", ["This table shows the cruel asymmetry of losses. A ten percent drawdown needs an eleven percent gain to recover. Twenty percent needs twenty five. Thirty percent needs about forty three. Forty percent needs sixty seven. Fifty percent needs a hundred percent, doubling your money just to get back to even. And a ninety percent drawdown needs nine hundred percent. The formula is one divided by one minus the drawdown, minus one. This is why protecting capital comes first.", "The takeaway: Gain needed = 1 ÷ (1 − drawdown) − 1: a 50% drawdown needs 100%.", "Pause and answer: Gain needed after a 40% drawdown?", "⏸", "Answer: 66.7%."]], ["losing-streaks-are-normal", ["Losing streaks are normal, and this table proves it. It shows the chance of at least one losing streak of a given length somewhere in a hundred trades. With a forty percent win rate, there's an eighty seven percent chance of six or more losses in a row, and almost a fifty fifty chance of eight. Even at a sixty percent win rate, there's a one in five chance of six straight losses. So a forty percent trend follower should fully expect six or more losses in a row. If you know that in advance, a streak won't shake your confidence or make you abandon a working system.", "The takeaway: Over 100 trades, long losing streaks are near certain at typical win rates.", "Pause and answer: 40% win rate: chance of at least 8 losses in a row in 100 trades?", "⏸", "Answer: About 49%."]], ["ten-straight-losses-at-each-risk-level", ["And here's what ten straight losses do at each risk level, compounded on the remaining balance. At half a percent per trade, you're down about five percent. At one percent, about ten percent. At two percent, about eighteen percent. At five percent, forty percent. And at ten percent per trade, sixty five percent of the account is gone. At one percent, a bad streak is an annoyance; at five to ten percent, it ends the account.", "The takeaway: At 1% risk ten losses cost about 10%; at 10% they cost about two-thirds.", "Pause and answer: Ten straight losses at 2% risk?", "⏸", "Answer: About −18%."]], ["portfolio-and-daily-limits", ["Portfolio and daily limits. Risk half a percent to two percent per trade, one percent being common and half a percent while learning. Cap total open risk at five to six percent across all positions, because all stops can hit on the same day. Treat correlated positions, like three IT stocks, as one position, because they fall together. Set a maximum daily loss of two to three percent, then stop trading for the day, which prevents revenge cascades. After a ten percent drawdown, halve your risk until you reach a new equity high. Keep leverage minimal while learning, using F and O only with defined risk. And reduce size or go flat before events like earnings, RBI policy, the budget or inflation data, because gaps jump past stops.", "The takeaway: 0.5–2% per trade, 5–6% max open risk, correlated positions counted as one, plus daily loss caps.", "Pause and answer: Typical max open risk across positions?", "⏸", "Answer: 5–6%."]]], "psych": [[null, ["Psychology is where most traders actually fail. They know the rules but don't follow them under pressure."]], ["biases-that-cost-money", ["The biases that cost money. Loss aversion makes you hold losers and cut winners early; counter it by setting the stop and target before entry. Confirmation bias makes you read only bullish views while you're long; write your invalidation level first. Recency bias says \"three wins in a row, I'm on a roll\"; keep the same size and rules every trade. Anchoring says \"I'll sell when it gets back to my price\"; use market levels, not your entry price. Overconfidence makes you size up after a streak; use a fixed risk percentage. FOMO makes you chase extended moves; no setup, no trade. And the sunk cost fallacy makes you average down to get back to even; judge each trade on its own.", "The takeaway: Loss aversion, confirmation, recency, overconfidence and anchoring each have a rule-based counter.", "Pause and answer: Counter to confirmation bias?", "⏸", "Answer: Write the invalidation level first."]], ["fear-greed-and-revenge", ["Fear makes you skip valid setups, close winners early, and move the stop to breakeven too soon. Greed makes you oversize, add without a plan, and hold past the target hoping for more. Revenge trading is forcing a trade right after a loss, often larger; the rule is thirty to sixty minutes off after an emotional loss. Overtrading comes from boredom or FOMO, so cap trades per day; \"no setup\" is a valid outcome. And here's the question to ask yourself in the moment: would I have written this exception into my plan when I was calm? If not, don't do it.", "The takeaway: Fear skips setups and cuts winners, greed oversizes, revenge forces trades after losses.", "Pause and answer: Name a fear behaviour.", "⏸", "Answer: Any of: skipping valid setups, closing winners early, moving the stop to breakeven too soon."]], ["written-trading-plan", ["A written trading plan covers the markets and timeframes you trade, your setups in one sentence each, your risk limits per trade, per day and in total, how you place orders and stops, your exits including partials, trailing and time stops, when not to trade, like the first fifteen minutes, before major news, after three losses, or when tired or upset, and how you review: a weekly journal review, with plan changes monthly, based on data.", "The takeaway: Markets and timeframes, exact setups, risk limits, order rules and review routines, written down.", "Pause and answer: Name three risk items in the plan.", "⏸", "Answer: Any three of: % per trade, max open risk, max daily loss, max trades per day."]], ["journal-and-performance-metrics", ["Keep a journal. For every trade, log the setup, timeframe, entry, stop, target, exit, profit or loss in rupees and in units of risk, your emotion, whether you followed the rules, and a screenshot. Weekly, review win rate, average result in risk units, the number of rule breaks, and your best and worst trades. Monthly, look at expectancy, maximum drawdown, profit factor and results by setup. When you study losers, ask: was it a bad setup, bad execution or bad luck? Only the first two need fixing. Change one thing at a time and judge it over at least twenty trades. Profit factor is gross profit divided by gross loss, and above one means you're profitable.", "The takeaway: Log every trade with R, emotion and rule adherence; review weekly and monthly.", "Pause and answer: Name three fields to log per trade.", "⏸", "Answer: Any three of: setup, timeframe, entry, stop, target, exit, P&L in ₹ and R, emotion, rule followed, screenshot."]]], "backtest": [[null, ["Backtesting tells you whether a rule had an edge in the past. Done badly, it tells you comforting lies, so this section is about doing it honestly."]], ["backtest-steps", ["The backtest steps. Write unambiguous rules for entry, stop, exit and size, because every vague word becomes a bug. Get enough data, five to ten years of daily bars, adjusted for splits and dividends, and free of survivorship bias if you're picking stocks. Simulate realistically: the signal comes on one bar, and the fill on the next bar's open, including brokerage, taxes like STT in India, the spread and slippage. Record every trade and compute the metrics. Validate out of sample, with walk forward tests, on other symbols, and with nearby parameter values. Then forward test on paper or at minimum size before scaling up.", "The takeaway: Unambiguous rules, clean adjusted data, signals on bar t filled on bar t+1, full costs, then metrics.", "Pause and answer: How much daily history is enough?", "⏸", "Answer: About 5–10 years, adjusted for splits and dividends."]], ["backtest-pitfalls", ["The pitfalls. Look ahead bias: generating the signal and filling at the same close, which is impossible in real life; fix it by shifting signals one bar. Survivorship bias: testing only today's index members, ignoring the companies that were delisted; use point in time constituents. Overfitting: finding that an RSI of seventeen with a stop of two point three seven ATRs \"works best\"; use few parameters and look for robust neighbourhoods. Ignoring costs: assume one or two ticks of slippage per side plus all charges. Unrealistic fills at the exact high or low: be conservative, and let price gap through your stops. And data snooping: trying hundreds of ideas and keeping the best one; hold out data from the very start and count how many things you tried.", "The takeaway: Look-ahead, survivorship and overfitting each inflate backtests; each has a fix.", "Pause and answer: Fix for look-ahead bias?", "⏸", "Answer: Shift signals one bar."]], ["metrics-to-report", ["Metrics to report. CAGR, the compound annual growth rate, which should beat buy and hold after costs. Maximum drawdown, which must be within your tolerance. The Sharpe ratio, average return over its volatility, annualised, where above one is good for a single strategy. The Sortino ratio, which only counts downside volatility. The MAR or Calmar ratio, CAGR divided by maximum drawdown, ideally above half. Profit factor, above one point three out of sample. Expectancy, positive over a hundred or more trades. And exposure, the share of time in the market, for context.", "The takeaway: CAGR, max drawdown, Sharpe and more; a strategy must beat buy-and-hold after costs.", "Pause and answer: Max drawdown formula?", "⏸", "Answer: min(equity ÷ running peak − 1)."]], ["walk-forward-testing", ["Walk forward testing simulates how you would really use a strategy. Optimise on, say, twenty fifteen to twenty eighteen, then test on twenty nineteen, data the optimisation never saw. Then roll forward: optimise on twenty sixteen to twenty nineteen and test on twenty twenty, and so on. Finally, stitch the test years together into one honest out of sample record. Rolling windows keep a fixed length, while anchored windows grow from one start date. Keep the in sample to out of sample ratio around three or five to one, with at least thirty trades per test window.", "The takeaway: Optimise on a window, test the next period, roll forward and stitch the test years.", "Pause and answer: After optimising 2015–2018, which year is tested?", "⏸", "Answer: 2019."]], ["walk-forward-efficiency-wfe", ["Walk forward efficiency compares the out of sample annual return with the in sample one. Above zero point seven is strong, meaning the strategy generalises well. Between half and zero point seven is acceptable decay. Between zero point three and a half is weak, suggesting overfitting or a strategy that depends on one regime. Below zero point three, or negative, means it's curve fit: don't trade it. Also require sixty to seventy percent of test windows to be profitable, and the best parameters should be stable from window to window.", "The takeaway: WFE = out-of-sample ÷ in-sample annual return; above 0.7 generalises well, below 0.3 is curve-fit.", "Pause and answer: WFE formula?", "⏸", "Answer: Annualised out-of-sample return ÷ annualised in-sample return."]], ["forward-testing", ["Forward testing is trading the strategy live on paper or at tiny size. How long depends on the style: two hundred to five hundred trades for scalping over a few weeks, a hundred to two hundred for intraday, fifty to a hundred for swing over a few months, and thirty to sixty for position trading over six to twelve months. If results are within twenty percent of the backtest, you're on track. Twenty to forty percent worse: investigate fills, rule breaks and the regime. More than forty percent worse, or negative expectancy: stop. And never retune during the test, because any change restarts the clock.", "The takeaway: Paper-trade enough trades for the style; results within 20% of the backtest are on track.", "Pause and answer: Minimum forward-test trades for swing trading?", "⏸", "Answer: About 50–100."]], ["a-vectorised-backtest-without-look-ahead-pandas", ["This is an honest backtest in pandas for a moving average crossover. The signal is one when the fast average is above the slow one. The crucial line shifts the signal by one bar to get the position, so today's signal only becomes tomorrow's position. That single shift separates an honest backtest from a fantasy. Daily strategy returns are the position times the market return, minus costs charged on every entry and exit. Equity compounds those returns, and from it you compute CAGR, maximum drawdown, Sharpe, the number of trades and exposure. The position size helper is the same formula as before: account times risk percentage, divided by the stop distance, rounded down. For ten lakh at one percent, entry five hundred and stop four eighty, it returns five hundred shares.", "The takeaway: Compute the signal on today's close, trade on the next bar, and subtract costs on position changes.", "Pause and answer: How do you avoid look-ahead in a vectorised backtest?", "⏸", "Answer: Shift the signal one bar before applying returns."]], ["robustness-checks", ["Robustness checks. Look for a parameter plateau: if a twenty period average works, eighteen and twenty two should work too, since a lone spike is noise. Test the same rules on other stocks or indices. Test bull, bear and sideways years separately. Use Monte Carlo reshuffling of trade order to see the range of possible drawdowns. And prefer rules with an economic reason behind them. A robust system winning fifty five percent at one and a half to one beats a fragile system winning seventy percent.", "The takeaway: Parameter plateaus, other markets, sub-periods and Monte Carlo reshuffles separate edge from noise.", "Pause and answer: What does a parameter plateau look like?", "⏸", "Answer: Neighbouring values (SMA 18, 20, 22) all work."]], ["tools", ["The tools. TradingView with Pine Script for quick visual checks. A custom pandas loop for full control and for learning exactly how fills work. Vectorbt for fast parameter scans across many symbols. Backtrader for event driven, complex order logic. And Amibroker, which is popular with Indian end of day traders.", "The takeaway: TradingView for quick checks, a pandas loop to learn fills, vectorbt for scans, backtrader for event-driven tests.", "Pause and answer: Which tool suits parameter scans across many symbols?", "⏸", "Answer: vectorbt."]]], "algo": [[null, ["Automation can enforce discipline, but it also lets mistakes happen at machine speed. So it comes with its own safety rules."]], ["from-hypothesis-to-live", ["The path from idea to live trading. Start with a hypothesis that has an economic reason. Write the rules in plain language. Backtest with costs over two hundred or more trades. Walk forward test, requiring an efficiency above half. Paper trade thirty to a hundred trades. Go live at minimum size. And only then scale, with hard limits and a kill switch.", "The takeaway: Hypothesis with a reason, plain-language rules, a costed backtest with 200+ trades, walk-forward WFE above 0.5, paper trading, then small live size.", "Pause and answer: Minimum backtest trades suggested?", "⏸", "Answer: 200 or more."]], ["quant-strategy-families", ["Quant strategy families. Trend following wins thirty five to forty five percent with big winners, and breaks in ranges. Mean reversion wins fifty five to seventy percent with smaller winners, and breaks in strong trends. Statistical arbitrage is market neutral, winning sixty to seventy five percent, and breaks when pairs decouple. Market making wins seventy to eighty five percent with small gains, and breaks in fast one way moves. For pairs trading, test for cointegration, a stable long run relationship between the two prices, and trade the z score of the spread.", "The takeaway: Trend following wins rarely but big; mean reversion wins often but small; stat arb is market-neutral until pairs decouple.", "Pause and answer: Typical trend-following win rate?", "⏸", "Answer: About 35–45%, with 2:1 to 5:1 wins."]], ["levels-of-automation", ["Levels of automation. Manual with a checklist: a script prints signals, and you decide and place orders. Semi automated: alerts or one click orders, and you supervise every trade. Fully automated: orders go through the broker's API and you handle only exceptions. Move up a level only after the strategy is validated and you understand partial fills, rejections and disconnects.", "The takeaway: Manual with a checklist, semi-automated with alerts, or fully automated with you handling exceptions.", "Pause and answer: What does semi-automated mean?", "⏸", "Answer: Alerts or one-click orders, with you supervising each trade."]], ["production-safety", ["Production safety. A kill switch that flattens all positions and halts at a daily loss limit. Hard limits in code: maximum position, maximum orders per minute and maximum daily loss. A heartbeat alert if the bot goes quiet. Reconnect logic for data feeds and token refreshes. Run it on a server, not a laptop. Use version control, separate development, paper and live environments, and never hot fix a live bot after a loss. And keep API keys out of your code and repositories.", "The takeaway: Kill switch, hard limits in code, heartbeats and reconnect logic keep a bot safe.", "Pause and answer: What does a kill switch do?", "⏸", "Answer: Flattens all positions and halts at a daily loss limit."]], ["apis-and-data-sources", ["APIs and data. In India, brokers like Zerodha Kite Connect, Upstox, Angel One, Dhan, Fyers and ICICI Breeze offer order APIs, and NSE publishes end of day bhavcopy data. Macro data comes from the RBI's database and MoSPI. Globally, Interactive Brokers, Alpaca and OANDA, with data from yfinance, Alpha Vantage, Polygon and FRED. One important regulatory note: SEBI's twenty twenty five framework for retail algorithmic trading routes algos through brokers, tags algo orders, requires registration above an orders per second threshold, and ties API access to registered static IP addresses. Rules change, so check the current circulars before automating anything.", "The takeaway: Indian broker APIs (Kite, Upstox, SmartAPI, Dhan, Fyers, Breeze) and data from broker history, NSE bhavcopy or vendors.", "Pause and answer: Name two Indian broker order APIs.", "⏸", "Answer: Any two of: Kite Connect, Upstox, Angel One SmartAPI, Dhan, Fyers, ICICI Breeze."]]], "mistakes": [[null, ["Let's go through the common mistakes, because learning from others' losses is much cheaper than learning from your own."]], ["mistake-consequence-fix", ["Mistakes, consequences and fixes. No written plan leads to random, emotional trades; write one page of rules. No stop, or moving it further away, lets one trade erase months of gains; place the stop before entry and only ever move it toward profit. Risking five to ten percent per trade turns normal streaks into disasters; risk half a percent to two percent. Averaging down builds a bigger position in the wrong direction; add only to winners. Trading against the higher timeframe means low odds setups. Chasing extended moves means buying the top of the candle. Indicator overload means contradictory signals. Trading into news or earnings means gaps through stops. Ignoring costs turns a gross edge into a net loss. Too many markets and timeframes means no depth anywhere. Revenge trading escalates losses; use a cool down rule and a maximum daily loss. No journal means repeating the same mistakes. And blaming \"manipulation\" means nothing gets learned.", "The takeaway: No plan, no stop, oversizing and revenge trading each have a predictable consequence and a simple fix.", "Pause and answer: Consequence of moving the stop further away?", "⏸", "Answer: One trade can erase months of gains."]], ["tips-worth-remembering", ["A few tips worth remembering. Price and volume come first, since indicators are derived from them. The best trades usually feel uncomfortable to take. A breakout that fails quickly is information: the other side is strong. Most money is made by sitting through trends, not by constantly entering and exiting. And: plan the trade, trade the plan, journal the result.", "The takeaway: Price and volume first, good trades feel uncomfortable, failed breakouts are information, money is made sitting through trends.", "Pause and answer: What does a quickly failing breakout tell you?", "⏸", "Answer: The other side is strong."]], ["combining-with-fundamentals", ["Technical and fundamental analysis work well together. Use fundamentals for what to trade: screen for quality and value, and only trade names you'd be willing to own. Use technicals for when: enter on a pullback or breakout, with a stop and target from the chart. And use options for how: to express a view or define risk, never to rescue a weak thesis. The fundamental analysis sheet covers the business side.", "The takeaway: Fundamentals choose what, technicals choose when, options choose how.", "Pause and answer: What role do fundamentals play here?", "⏸", "Answer: Screening for quality and value: what to trade."]]], "last": [[null, ["Finally, the checklists. Run these before every session and every trade."]], ["before-the-session", ["Before the session. What's the market regime on my timeframe: trend, range or squeeze? Have I marked the higher timeframe bias and key levels? Have I checked the calendar for results, RBI policy, inflation data, the budget and expiry? Have I noted India VIX, institutional flows and global cues? Am I calm and rested, with no revenge or FOMO? And have I set today's maximum loss and maximum number of trades?", "The takeaway: Regime, higher-timeframe bias and levels, the event calendar, VIX and flows, and your own state.", "Pause and answer: Name two calendar events to check.", "⏸", "Answer: Any two of: results, RBI policy, CPI, the Budget, expiry."]], ["before-each-trade", ["Before each trade. Can I state the setup in one sentence, and does it match my written plan? Is it at a level or structure point, not in the middle of a range? Are the entry, stop and target defined before I place the order? Is the size the risk amount divided by the stop distance, rounded down? Is the reward to risk at least one and a half to two after costs? And is there no major event before my planned exit?", "The takeaway: Plan-matching setup at a level, entry, stop and target defined, size from the stop, R:R at least 2.", "Pause and answer: Where should the setup sit?", "⏸", "Answer: At a level or structure point, not mid-range."]], ["during-and-after", ["During and after. Place the stop order immediately, and never move it against the trade. Don't add to losers; take partials and trail per the plan. If you're stopped out, accept it, with no immediate emotional re entry. Log the setup, entry, exit, result in risk units, emotion and whether you followed the rules. Review weekly and monthly.", "The takeaway: Stop placed immediately, never moved against you, no adding to losers, accept stops, log everything.", "Pause and answer: Can you move a stop further away?", "⏸", "Answer: Never against the trade."]], ["one-line-formulas", ["The one line formulas, to keep in your head. Shares equal account times risk percent, divided by the distance from entry to stop. Reward to risk is target minus entry, over entry minus stop. Break even win rate is one over one plus the reward to risk. Expectancy is win rate times average win minus loss rate times average loss. The gain needed to recover from a drawdown is one over one minus the drawdown, minus one. And an ATR stop sits one and a half to three ATRs from the entry.", "The takeaway: Shares, R:R, break-even win rate, expectancy, recovery and ATR stop on one card.", "Pause and answer: Break-even win rate formula?", "⏸", "Answer: 1 ÷ (1 + R)."]], ["educational-note", ["And one last reminder. This sheet is a study reference built from personal study notes and standard textbooks. It isn't investment advice. Pattern statistics are historical averages from US stocks, and trading carries a real risk of loss, so check the current NSE, BSE and SEBI rules before acting on anything here. Study well, trade small while you learn, and protect your capital first."]], ["numbers-that-expire", ["The numbers that expire card collects every figure on this sheet that a regulator, exchange or budget can change, like lot sizes, tax rates, STT and expiry days. Each one shows its current value and when it last changed, with a link to the source. Use it as a checklist: before you trade on any number from this sheet, check it there, and then check the source, because these move."]]]};
